Today

Functions to manipulate a data frame;
Summaries by group;
Import and clean;
Combining tables, wide and long format.

The pipe |&gt;
Read |&gt; as “and then”. Shortcut: Ctrl/Cmd + Shift + M.

penguins |&gt;
  filter(sex == "female") |&gt;
  select(species, body_mass) |&gt;
  mutate(body_mass_kg = body_mass / 1000) |&gt;
  arrange(body_mass_kg)

# A tibble: 165 × 3
  species   body_mass body_mass_kg
  &lt;fct&gt;         &lt;int&gt;        &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Chinstrap      2700         2.7 
2 Adelie         2850         2.85
3 Adelie         2850         2.85
4 Adelie         2900         2.9 
5 Adelie         2900         2.9 
# ℹ 160 more rows


x |&gt; f() is the same as f(x). Older code uses %&gt;%: same thing.

Functions

name &lt;- function(arg1, arg2 = default) {
  # body
  result          # last value is returned
}


The standard error of the mean, \(s / \sqrt{n}\):

se &lt;- function(x) {
  sd(x) / sqrt(length(x))
}
se(c(512, 430, 601, 488, 550))

[1] 28.78958

Why reports from code?
The copy-paste workflow:

run the script;
copy numbers into Word, paste plots;
a reviewer asks to exclude 3 subjects… redo it all by hand.


A Quarto document (.qmd) holds text, code and (after rendering) results:

every number is computed when the document is created;
new data? Click Render and everything updates.

10. By hemisphere

by_hemisphere &lt;- happy |&gt;
  group_by(hemisphere) |&gt;
  summarise(median_total = median(total_per_capita)) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(median_total))
by_hemisphere

# A tibble: 3 × 2
  hemisphere median_total
  &lt;chr&gt;             &lt;dbl&gt;
1 north               306
2 south               243
3 both                239


The highest median is in the hemisphere labelled north: 306 servings per person per year.
